{
 "id": "DW-03",
 "system": "ac3",
 "sessionId": "bm-ac2-dw-03-d244c0fb6c",
 "endReason": "concluded",
 "customerTurns": 6,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "dishwaher leakin water all over the floor and the plug sockets are right next to it whats going on"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Water near the plug or socket is dangerous. Please don't touch the plug, socket or machine if they or your hands are wet. If you can do it safely, switch off the circuit at the consumer unit (fuse box), keep away from the water, and don't use the machine again until a qualified electrician or appliance engineer has checked it. Leaking water onto the floor usually indicates a failed door seal, a cracked tub, or a loose hose connection. Since plug sockets are nearby, please ensure the area is dry and do not touch any electrical components while water is present to avoid shock hazards. From what you have described, the most likely cause is is there water in the base, and where is it leaking from (find the source first). Is water leaking from the side of the door during washing, or has the machine stopped / gone into anti-flood with the drain pump running and water underneath rather than on the floor?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "its coming from the bottom of the door while its washing, theres loads of suds and bubbles. water is spreading toward an extension lead on the floor and its still running. what do i do"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Turn off the washing machine immediately at the mains plug. Move the extension lead away from the water to prevent electric shock or fire. From what you have described, the most likely cause is too much (or the wrong) detergent foaming over. Too much detergent, or one that isn't low-foam (HE), can foam over and push water out. How much detergent do you use, and is it a low-foam (HE) washing-machine detergent?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "we ran out of tablets so my housemate squirted fairy washing up liquid in. its a dishwasher not washing machine btw. is it ok to turn it off at the plug the socket is next to it and the floor is wet"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, you should switch it off immediately because water near an extension lead creates a serious electric shock hazard. Do not touch the plug with wet hands; use dry pliers if necessary or cut power at the main fuse box if you cannot reach the socket safely. From what you have described, the most likely cause is foam from washing-up liquid or the wrong detergent. Next: Stop or cancel the cycle immediately. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ok i turned it off at the plug and moved the extension lead. tub was full of suds when i opened the door. what now"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is too much (or the wrong) detergent foaming over. Next: Run a rinse/drain cycle with no detergent to clear the foam; let suds settle before draining. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "wont running it again make more suds and leak again? the floor is still wet and its the landlords machine. should i just leave it off?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, running the machine will almost certainly create more suds and cause further leaking because the root cause is excessive detergent foam. You must stop using the appliance until the issue is resolved to prevent water damage and electrical hazards. Is it foam (not water) escaping the drawer/door, and does it keep adding rinses or refuse to spin?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "its suds coming out the door bottom, not the drawer, its a dishwasher. so i just leave it off and tell the landlord? what about the wet floor"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, you should inform your landlord immediately as this is their responsibility. For the wet floor, use towels to soak up the water and ensure the area around the extension lead is completely dry to prevent electrical hazards. From what you have described, the most likely cause is foam from washing-up liquid or the wrong detergent. Next: Let the foam settle, then scoop or wipe it. Let me know what you find."
  }
 ],
